\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
We test this with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs into \textsc{entailment}, \textsc{contradiction}, or \textsc{neutral}. In combo mode, the NLI verdict is integrated with SC's $K$ votes via weight $w$: each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$), because NLI classification requires (premise, conclusion) pairs that these datasets naturally provide; multiple-choice datasets (LogiQA) lack this structure.

\paragraph{Main result.}
DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo mode yields $+1.96$\,pp at $w{=}1$ and $+2.94$\,pp at $w{=}3$ (both n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n = 600$), $w{=}3$ achieves $+3.50$\,pp ($p = 0.013$) and $w{=}5$ reaches $+10.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$)---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp, raw $p = 0.014$; BH adj.\ $p = 0.112$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). We report $w{=}3$ as primary; $w$ is a dataset-specific hyperparameter ($w{=}5$ yields $+10.67$\,pp on Mistral-7B but only $+1.67$\,pp n.s.\ on LLaMA-3.1-8B), and higher weights risk overfitting. After Benjamini--Hochberg correction ($m{=}6$), ProofWriter $w{=}3$ remains significant (adjusted $p = 0.038$) and $w{=}5$ ($p < 0.001$); FOLIO comparisons are non-significant. Per-label caveat: DeBERTa over-predicts \textsc{False} on ProofWriter (67.8\%) and performs poorly on \textsc{Unknown} (3.5\% accuracy), as NLI's ``neutral'' fails to capture logical unknowability.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
The 406M encoder-only DeBERTa and 7B decoder-only Mistral share no architecture, training data, or inference mechanism, satisfying the independence condition that same-model sampling cannot provide (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). Per-label complementarity emerges: NLI excels on \textsc{False} (71.43\% vs.\ SC's 38.10\%), SC dominates \textsc{Unknown} (68.12\% vs.\ 18.84\%). All three NLI architectures tested (DeBERTa, RoBERTa, BART) produce significant improvements at $w \geq 3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}).

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
We evaluate four generators from three model families spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with the identical DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone). Combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability (Spearman $\rho = 0.970$, $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}): Mistral-7B (SC${=}$39.33\%, gap${=}{+}15.67$) gains $+3.50$\,pp ($p{=}0.013$) and LLaMA-3.1-8B (SC${=}$70.00\%, gap${=}{-}15.00$) achieves $+4.17$\,pp ($p{=}0.0035$), while Qwen2.5-14B and Qwen3-14B plateau at ${\leq}1.00$\,pp (n.s.). NLI escape persists even when the verifier is moderately weaker in standalone accuracy, but vanishes as the capability gap widens (${\leq}{-}18.5$\,pp). Sigmoid and linear fits yield similar adjusted $R^2$ ($0.781$ vs.\ $0.752$; $\Delta = 0.029$), so we report the monotonic trend rather than a specific inflection point. Two of four generators---from distinct model families---show statistically significant improvement (both surviving Benjamini--Hochberg correction at $m{=}12$), ruling out generator-specific confounds.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\columnwidth]{figures/capability_gap_scatter}
\vspace{-3mm}
\caption{Combo gain vs.\ capability gap (verifier standalone $-$ generator SC) at $w{=}3$. Gain decreases monotonically with generator capability (Spearman $\rho = 0.970$, $p < 0.001$); significant gains persist into moderate negative gaps.}
\label{fig:capability-gap}
\vspace{-6mm}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-generator NLI escape with identical DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone, ProofWriter $n{=}600$). Combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability (Spearman $\rho = 0.970$) across four models from three families.}
\label{tab:cross_generator_nli}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Generator & Family & SC & Gap & Combo $\Delta$ ($w{=}3$) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & Mistral & 39.33\% & $+$15.67 & $+$3.50\,pp & 0.013 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & LLaMA & 70.00\% & $-$15.00 & $+$4.17\,pp & 0.0035 \\
Qwen2.5-14B$^\dagger$ & Qwen & 73.50\% & $-$18.50 & $+$0.83\,pp & 0.41 \\
Qwen3-14B & Qwen & 85.00\% & $-$30.00 & $+$1.00\,pp & 0.15 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\par\vspace{1mm}
{\footnotesize $^\dagger$SC from combo trace set; Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports 75.17\% (same seed, different server).}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale and language.}
All models range from 7B to 32B parameters; whether the independence bottleneck persists at larger scales (70B+) or with reasoning-specialized models is untested. All datasets are English-language logical reasoning benchmarks with discrete answer spaces. We do not evaluate trained symbolic extractors, external solvers with verified grounding, or multilingual settings. Oracle analysis is limited to FOLIO and ProofWriter (gold annotations available); extending to commonsense reasoning requires constructing comparable gold constraint sets. The cross-architecture NLI verifier (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}) provides no benefit on LogiQA (combo $\Delta \leq +1$\,pp, all $p > 0.5$), indicating that cross-architecture escape requires task-verifier alignment---NLI is effective only when the task naturally decomposes into premise-conclusion entailment. Even on compatible tasks, NLI gain vanishes when the capability gap is large (Qwen3-14B ProofWriter: SC 85\% vs.\ DeBERTa 55\%, gap $= -30$\,pp, $\Delta \leq +1.00$\,pp, n.s.), though moderate negative gaps still yield significant gains (LLaMA-3.1-8B: gap $= -15$\,pp, $+4.17$\,pp, $p = 0.0035$; \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}). Scaling the cross-architecture approach---via trained verifiers, code-based symbolic execution, or multi-verifier ensembles---remains unexplored and may extend the escape to stronger generators and broader task types.

\paragraph{Statistical power.}
The per-label complementarity analysis of the cross-architecture NLI result (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}) involves small cell sizes (e.g., 42 \textsc{False} problems, 69 \textsc{Unknown} problems); per-label McNemar tests would be underpowered. The bias ratio metric ($4.97\times$) was initially computed on a single condition (Mistral-7B FOLIO, $n = 204$); cross-model replication (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}) confirms the pattern but is limited to FOLIO. Four-seed replication shows 2 of 4 seeds yielding null or negative results, and the intermediate-SC region is narrow (SC $\approx$ 50--55\%). The process-level similarity metric uses TF-IDF representations (a lower bound on true process similarity). Pairwise Pearson $r$ between traces (mean $0.625$, Mistral-7B) is monotonically related to Fleiss' $\kappa$ across all four model families, supporting $\kappa$ as a valid consensus proxy despite the simplifying assumptions of Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}.

\paragraph{Computational cost trade-off.}
SICA requires $4.5\times$ the wall-clock time of SC (81.4 vs.\ 17.7\,min for 204 questions at $K{=}12$), with constraint extraction accounting for 76\% of overhead (\S\ref{sec:exp-setup}). This $3.5\times$ additional cost yields no significant accuracy gain on any condition, making the method impractical in its current form. Alternative prompting strategies (few-shot, chain-of-thought extraction) might improve extraction quality, though the oracle gap suggests the bottleneck is structural. Additional limitations regarding power analysis and deduplication strategy are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations-extended}.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} All code, data, and experimental scripts will be released upon publication to facilitate replication of SICA, the oracle controls, and all remediation experiments.

